---
// "What we collect and why": the full notice behind "How we review applications" (CLAUDE.md non-negotiable 4,
// docs/research/legal.md). It describes what this build collects, and nothing it does not. When its substance changes,
// change NOTICE_VERSION in lib/apply.ts too: every application records the version its candidate was shown.
import CareersLayout from '../../layouts/CareersLayout.astro';
import { getPublicOrganization } from '../../lib/api';
import { NOTICE_VERSION } from '../../lib/apply';
import { useEmbedding } from '../../lib/embed';
import { basePath } from '../../lib/host';

const response = await getPublicOrganization(Astro.params.organization!);
if (response.status !== 200) {
  return new Response(null, { status: 404 });
}

const employer = response.data;
const base = basePath(Astro.request, employer.slug);
useEmbedding(Astro.locals, Astro.url, employer.embedOrigins);

// The headings below, in order: a phone gets a page of about five screens, so a list of them saves the scrolling.
const sections = [
  { id: 'browsing', title: 'While you look at jobs' },
  { id: 'applying', title: 'When you apply' },
  { id: 'saving', title: 'If you save to finish later' },
  { id: 'after', title: 'After you apply' },
  { id: 'your-page', title: 'Your page' },
  { id: 'future-roles', title: 'Future roles' },
  { id: 'checks', title: 'What we check, and what we never do' },
  { id: 'assistive', title: 'Assistive technology' },
  { id: 'ai', title: 'AI tools' },
  { id: 'rights', title: 'Your choices' },
];
---

<CareersLayout
  title={`What we collect and why | ${employer.name}`}
  description={`How ${employer.name} and ScreenHire handle your information when you apply for a job.`}
  organizationName={employer.name}
  organizationSlug={employer.slug}
  branding={employer.branding}
>
  <article class="flex flex-col gap-6 text-candidate-body leading-relaxed [&_section_h2]:font-display [&_section_h2]:text-xl [&_section_h2]:font-bold [&_section_li]:mt-1 [&_section_ul]:list-disc [&_section_ul]:pl-5">
    <header>
      <h1 class="font-display text-3xl leading-tight font-bold tracking-tight">What we collect and why</h1>
      <p class="mt-2 text-candidate-muted-foreground">
        {employer.name} uses ScreenHire to receive applications. {employer.name} decides how applications are used;
        ScreenHire handles them on its behalf.
      </p>
    </header>

    <nav aria-labelledby="contents-heading" class="rounded-2xl border border-candidate-border bg-candidate-surface px-4 py-3.5">
      <h2 id="contents-heading" class="text-base font-bold">On this page</h2>
      <ul class="mt-1.5 grid gap-x-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {sections.map((each) => (
          <li><a href={`#${each.id}`} class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center text-link">{each.title}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <section aria-labelledby="browsing">
      <h2 id="browsing">While you look at jobs</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Only our server logs: your network (IP) address, your browser's name, the page and the time. We use them to keep
        the site running and secure, and delete them within 30 days. There are no tracking cookies, no analytics and no
        device fingerprinting on these pages.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="applying">
      <h2 id="applying">When you apply</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Nothing is collected until you start on the form, below the notice that says what we check. A file goes to storage
        as soon as you choose it, and one that is never sent with an application is deleted after a day. With an
        application we keep:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>what you enter: your name, email, phone number, answers and the files you upload;</li>
        <li>the network (IP) address and browser you sent it from;</li>
        <li>information about how the form was used, unless you switch on the assistive technology option;</li>
        <li>
          a device code, where the job allows it (in some countries only if you tick the box for it): a one-way code made
          from your browser's settings;
        </li>
        <li>technical signals your browser reports about itself;</li>
        <li>whether you switched on the assistive technology option;</li>
        <li>your answer about AI tools, and anything you tell us about how you used them;</li>
        <li>what our checks read from your files and answers, and their results;</li>
        <li>
          if you apply through the form on {employer.name}'s own website, which website that was (the form is still ours,
          and works the same);
        </li>
        <li>which version of this notice you were shown.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-2">
        We delete the network address, browser, information about how the form was used and the device code within 30 days,
        and that information from a form you never send within a day. What we keep from them is coarse: the network's operator and country,
        never your address or a location more precise than a country.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="saving">
      <h2 id="saving">If you save to finish later</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        You can save the form and come back to it. We keep your name, email, phone number and answers, whether you switched
        on the assistive technology option and your answer about AI tools under your email address, and email you a link that only works for you. We never keep your files or information about how the form was used: attach your files again when you come back. Nobody at {employer.name} sees what you saved until you
        send it. We delete it when you send your application, or 30 days after you first saved it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="after">
      <h2 id="after">After you apply</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        {employer.name} may email you a link to a few more questions. The link works for 14 days and only for you. That
        page keeps your answers and nothing else: no information about how the form was used, no device code and no checks.
        You can save your answers there to finish later through the same link; we delete them when you send them, or 30
        days after you first saved them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="your-page">
      <h2 id="your-page">Your page</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        {employer.name} may email you a link to your own page, and every email about an application ends with one. You can
        also ask for the link on <a href={`${base}/my-applications`} class="text-link">the page for your applications</a>,
        with the address you applied with. The page shows your applications to {employer.name}, a saved application you can
        finish, anything waiting for you, and the messages between you and the team. The link works for a limited time and only
        for you, so do not forward the email. If you write a reply on that page, with or without files, we keep it with your
        application like a reply by email, and the team can read it. The page keeps what you type and nothing else: no
        information about how the page was used, no device code and no checks. We use the address you give there only to
        find your applications and to send the link.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="future-roles">
      <h2 id="future-roles">Future roles</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        At the end of the form you can tick "Keep my details for future roles". It is up to you, and it is never ticked for
        you. If you tick it, {employer.name} keeps your application until you ask, or until {employer.name} removes it,
        and may email you an invitation to apply for another role. Every invitation has a link that stops this at once.
        When you stop it, or the team removes you, {employer.name} no longer keeps your details for future roles or
        invites you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="checks">
      <h2 id="checks">What we check, and what we never do</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        We check every application for signs of bots and impersonation, because they crowd out real candidates. The checks
        use the information listed under <a href="#applying">When you apply</a>: how the application arrived, whether its
        details hold up, and whether they repeat on other applications to {employer.name}. Each check is one piece of
        evidence. None of them is a judgement on its own.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        The site may ask your browser to complete a bot challenge from Cloudflare, which then sees your network address and
        browser. The device code is made differently for every employer, so it never matches across them.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        If {employer.name} has turned it on, an AI model from our provider compares the facts across what you send and shows
        a person where they differ. It is never asked whether you used AI to write them. Our provider is not allowed to use
        your information to train its models. A person decides what any difference means, and there is often an ordinary
        explanation.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-2">
        <li>Our checks for bots and impersonation never reject you. A person at {employer.name} looks at anything they flag.</li>
        <li>We never give you a score or a percentage, and never guess at your feelings, honesty or personality.</li>
        <li>We never record which keys you press, and never use your camera or microphone.</li>
        <li>We never look up your social media profiles. We only use what you give us.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="assistive">
      <h2 id="assistive">Assistive technology</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        If you use dictation, a screen reader, switch access or autofill, switch on the assistive technology option at the
        start of the form. We then turn off the checks on how the form is used. Using the option is never held against you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="ai">
      <h2 id="ai">AI tools</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Each job says whether {employer.name} welcomes AI help with your application. We ask you about it at the end of
        the form, and a person reads your answer alongside your application.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="rights">
      <h2 id="rights">Your choices</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        You can ask {employer.name} to see, correct or delete your information, and to have a person review anything we
        flagged about your application.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        To ask for a copy of your information, or to ask for it to be erased, use <a href={`${base}/data-request`} class="text-link">the page for data requests</a>.
        We email a link to the address you applied with, and the link opens the page where you make the request. The page where
        you enter your address may ask your browser to complete the same bot challenge from Cloudflare. The team at
        {employer.name} decides what to do with each request.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        To correct something, to ask for a review, or if you cannot use that page, write to {employer.name} through the email
        address they use to write to you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="text-candidate-hint text-candidate-muted-foreground">Notice version <span class="font-mono">{NOTICE_VERSION}</span></p>
  </article>
</CareersLayout>
